**Read:** [Nano-to-Macro Transport Processes — MIT 2.57](../../../physics/thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/sources/Nano-to-Macro-Transport-MIT-2-57.lingua#r_1YF7TD689ZRGQ51D4B3NGVHZQG), the complete relevant graduate transport and measurement sequence; [Functional material response — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Functional-Response-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_6WMQKCE9PFYYETKPTR50DH6RTM), dielectric/ferroelectric and coupled-response packages; the original thermal and electromagnetic method sources.

Choose a heterogeneous material or film stack with heat transport and electrical insulation requirements. Derive its boundary-value and measurement models. Infer parameters from one set of observations, then compare predictions at changed thickness, frequency, temperature or boundary conditions.

Investigate whether an apparent dielectric enhancement or thermal resistance belongs to the bulk, an interface or the measurement. Include a microstructural alternative and an experiment that could distinguish it. Close the energy account and keep field-amplitude, temperature and timescale conventions explicit.

**Deliver:** A complete source-method reconstruction, independently checked limiting solutions, sensitivity/identifiability analysis and a defended material comparison. Separate simulation, published observations and any new measurements actually performed. Revise a claim that remains underdetermined.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
**Continue in materials engineering:** [Defend the functional material account](Defend-the-functional-material-account.lingua#r_4R7JDE29DY1T4CNRV4Y6PDYHM9).

